    "doc_text": "AFP_ENG_20051215.0020\n\nKARACHI, Dec 15 , 2005\n\nEngland win toss, put Pakistan into bat in third one-dayer\n\n\n\n\nEngland captain Marcus Trescothick won his third consecutive toss and put Pakistan into bat in the third day-night limited overs international at the National stadium here on Thursday.\n\nThe series is levelled at 1-1 after England won the first match by 42 runs, while Pakistan won the second by seven wickets -- both played in Lahore.\n\nEngland lost batsman Kevin Pietersen two days before the match when back and rib cage problems forced him to return home.\n\nHis place was taken by Vikram Solanki, who was super-sub in Monday's second match.\n\nPakistan included allrounder Shahid Afridi in place of leg-spinner Danish Kaneria from their line-up of the first two matches.\n\nAfridi served a ban of one Test and two one-day matches over damaging the pitch during the second Test at Faisalabad.\n\nEngland: Marcus Trescothick (capt), Andrew Strauss, Paul Collingwood, Andrew Flintoff, Vikram Solanki, Ian Blackwell, Geraint Jones, Matthew Prior, Steve Harmison, James Anderson, Liam Plunkett. Super-sub: Ian Bell\n\nPakistan: Inzamam-ul-Haq (capt), Salman Butt, Shoaib Malik, Younis Khan, Mohammad Yousuf, Abdul Razzaq, Kamran Akmal, Mohammad Sami, Shahid Afridi, Shoaib Akhtar, Rana Naved-ul-Hasan. Super-sub: Yasir Arafat\n\nUmpires: Rudi Koertzen (RSA) and Tony Hill (NZL)\n\nTV umpire: Zameer Haider (PAK)\n\nMatch referee: Jeff Crowe (NZL)",
